    principle: "Know the ancient Near Eastern world",
    explanation: "Israel lived among Egypt, Babylon, Canaan and Assyria, and the Old Testament often speaks in their language and forms, sometimes to challenge them. Knowing that world helps us see what was ordinary and what was radical. Scholars such as John Walton have helped many readers see this, though some of their specific proposals are debated. Context protects us from both over-reading and under-reading the text.",
    example: "Babylonian creation stories like the Enuma Elish depict humans made as slaves to serve quarrelling gods. Genesis 1 instead says every human, not only kings, is made ‘in God’s image’ (Genesis 1:27), and God rests in a good creation. Read against its world, Genesis 1 is a stunning statement of human dignity.",
